<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paraná River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paraná is the second-longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, flowing about 4,880 kilometres from south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> along the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> river and form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. With its tributary the Paraguay it was the main route of Spanish settlement in the interior, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, and it remains a vital waterway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parana-river.jpg" alt="Paraná River" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chocofrito · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuenca_Paran%C3%A1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Paraná takes its name from Guarani words meaning river or sea, and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> has an immense number of river names containing para- or parana-. The river forms at the confluence of the Paranaíba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and runs southwest for about 619 kilometres to the city of Saltos del Guaira in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. For roughly the next 200 kilometres it forms the boundary between Paraguay and Brazil, up to the confluence with the Iguazu River, and after that it is the border between Paraguay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>.</p><p>It turns west for some 820 kilometres and meets the Paraguay River, its largest tributary, then runs south for about 820 kilometres through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and turns east near Rosario. Below the city of Diamante it splits into several arms and forms the Paraná Delta. Its final stretch, under 500 kilometres, ends at the Uruguay River, where the two form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first European to go up the Paraná was the Venetian explorer Sebastian Cabot, who did so in 1526 while working for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Between 1973 and 1984 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itaipu-dam">Itaipu Dam</a> was built, creating a massive, shallow reservoir. It flooded the Guaíra Falls, the Sete Quedas, a series of seven cascades once said to rival the Iguazu Falls. A second major hydroelectric project, the Yacyretá Dam, was built jointly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The dams blocked ship navigation to cities further upstream, and the rising river flooded the lower parts of Encarnación.</p><p>Navigation became a matter of dispute between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. Argentina wished to build a bridge across the river, while Paraguay would facilitate it only if the navigation issue was settled. A bilateral agreement on a bridge was signed in October 1964, and an agreement endorsing free navigation, requiring each nation to treat the other's vessels as its own, was signed in January 1973. Water-level records exist from 1904, because the river is used by oceangoing ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Paraná and its tributaries drain much of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, including nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. The basin contains large cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, Rosario, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a>. Much of the river is navigable as part of the Paraná–Paraguay and Tietê–Paraná waterways, linking inland cities in Argentina and Paraguay with the ocean. Fishing is a livelihood along its banks, and the delta is a well-known place for watching birds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paraná River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their traditional range lies in what is now Paraguay between the <b>Paraná River</b> and the lower Paraguay River, the Misiones Province of Argentina, southern Brazil once as far east as Rio de Janeiro, and parts of Uruguay and Bolivia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/guarani">Guaraní · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The northern part, with its capital at Asunción, became the Governorate of Paraguay, also known as Guayrá, covering the lands along the Paraguay and upper <b>Paraná</b> rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-paraguay">Governorate of Paraguay</a></li></ul></section>
